research_questions.method_sketch:
1. Twin construction. For every annotated frame, render a twin with Blender Cycles or SAPIEN. Use:
   - the dataset's own intrinsics and extrinsics;
   - MANO, or a NIMBLE fit as Zhao et al. did, with scanned hand textures;
   - YCB or dataset object meshes;
   - a background plate inpainted from hand-free frames of the same sequence.
2. Factor ladder, trained synthetic-only at each rung:
   - S0: clean twin;
   - S1: plus real background plate;
   - S2: plus forearm and sleeve;
   - S3: plus simulated depth, comparing heuristic noise, the open-source physics-based active-stereo simulator in SAPIEN (T-RO, arXiv 2201.11924) and the RQ2 model;
   - S4: contact dose-response, re-rendering the same frames with controlled penetration or gap (2, 5, 10 mm) and finger-placement noise, or with grasps replaced by synthesized ones (e.g. DexGraspNet or GraspXL).
3. Paired modality swaps on identical frames: real RGB with twin depth, twin RGB with real depth.
4. Ground-truth noise floor. Compute the point-to-mesh residual of real depth against the annotated hand and object meshes. Report results on a low-residual subset to separate model error from label error.
5. Models: three representative families, all trained under each condition:
   - an RGB joint hand-and-object regressor;
   - a depth or point-cloud tracker in the style of HOTrack (arXiv 2209.12009);
   - a lightweight render-and-compare object refiner.
6. Cross-dataset protocol: train on the DexYCB twin, test on real H2O and HO-Cap, to expose dataset-specific randomisation.
7. Report the share of the gap closed per factor, with confidence intervals over seeds.

research_questions.minimum_experiment:
Scope: DexYCB only, one camera serial, default S0 split, about 40-60k frames.

Six conditions:
- real-only;
- clean twin RGB-D;
- twin plus real background plate;
- twin plus SAPIEN active-stereo depth;
- the two modality swaps.

Model: one lightweight RGB-D joint model (ResNet-50 RGB branch, depth branch, MANO head and YCB object-pose head), 3 seeds per condition.

Metrics: PA-MPJPE, object ADD-S AUC binned by hand-occlusion level, penetration depth, contact F1 computed from the meshes, and the ground-truth residual floor.

Success criterion: either the named factors explain at least about 70 percent of the object-pose gap, or one factor clearly dominates (for example depth artifacts at the hand-object boundary).

Estimated effort: 6-8 weeks for one student.

research_questions.question:
Can a sensor-conditioned depth corruption model reproduce the close-range artifacts specific to hands well enough for synthetic-only training to work? The model would be learned from paired rendered and real depth of annotated hand-object frames. The artifacts are:
- missing and flying pixels on thin fingers;
- the projector shadow the hand casts on the held object;
- material-dependent error on skin versus object surfaces.
The test is whether a depth or RGB-D hand-object model trained only on synthetic data then gets within 10 percent of real-only training on real benchmarks. Does the model also transfer from active-stereo sensors to time-of-flight and other consumer RGB-D sensors?

research_questions.method_sketch:
1. Setting. Seen objects, synthetic-only, on YCB objects, so compute stays small and real tests exist in DexYCB and HO-3D. Unseen-object retraining is out of scope: per their GitHub READMEs, checked 2026-10-02, MegaPose and FoundationPose release training data but not training code.
2. Base data: the BOP YCB-V PBR renders.
3. Training conditions, five in total:
   - (a) no hands;
   - (b) cut-and-paste random hand occluders;
   - (c) MANO hands with synthesized grasps (DexGraspNet, GraspXL or a GraspIt ObMan-style pipeline), not physically refined;
   - (d) contact-consistent grasps: penetration-free, physics-refined, with forearm and approach pose;
   - (e) condition (d) plus a hand mask or hand mesh given as model input.
4. Contact dose-response: inject 2, 5 and 10 mm penetration into condition (d).
5. Models: a GDRNPP-style regressor (public training code, not verified here) and a small DeepIM-style render-and-compare refiner.
6. Evaluation:
   - real DexYCB and HO-3D v3 object poses, binned by hand-occlusion ratio;
   - frozen MegaPose or FoundationPose inference with and without hand-mask conditioning as unseen-object reference points on BOP-H3 subsets whose objects fit.

research_questions.data_and_hardware:
Public data:
- BOP YCB-V PBR, YCB meshes and MANO;
- grasp synthesizers DexGraspNet and GraspXL (code public; check licenses);
- real test sets: DexYCB and HO-3D v3;
- optional: the BOP-H3 datasets via the BOP toolkit.

Hardware: one GPU. Rendering is about 1-2 GPU-days per condition and training a few GPU-days per model (estimates, not measured).

research_questions.minimum_experiment:
Three public datasets with motion-capture or multi-view ground truth. (a) HO-Cap (arXiv 2406.06843): multi-view RGB-D with handovers; use single camera views. (b) DexYCB: RGB-D, 8 views, short grasps; serves as an easy control. (c) A subset of HOT3D (CVPR 2025, 833 minutes, 3.7M images, motion-capture ground truth for hands and objects). It is RGB/monochrome only with no per-frame depth, so it serves the RGB trackers and the long-horizon drift metric. Trackers, at least six:
- FoundationPose in tracking mode;
- FoundationPose with periodic re-estimation;
- BundleTrack or BundleSDF;
- ICG;
- an RGB tracker (RGBTrack, IROS 2025, or ComPose or GRC-Pose if code is released);
- Point2Pose or RRTrack if code is released.
A paper-worthy outcome has three parts. First, at least one ranking inversion between per-frame AUC and continuity F-score. Second, a quantified silent-failure rate that grows with occlusion duration and is worst during handovers. Third, a released evaluation toolkit with per-frame visibility and grasp-state labels for all three datasets.

Evaluate on HO-Cap single RGB-D views (handovers) and DexYCB, plus HOT3D in an RGB-only variant, all with the continuity protocol. Baselines: FoundationPose, FoundationPose plus re-estimation on loss, last-pose hold, Point2Pose or RRTrack if code exists, and ComPose if code exists. Ablations: rigid carry without uncertainty; carry with fixed-growth uncertainty; carry with learned slip; with and without conformal verification; with and without handover switching. Headline claims needed:
- higher tracking recall and lower error on frames inside occlusions longer than about 1 s;
- shorter time-to-recover and fewer silent failures after reappearance;
- 90 percent predicted intervals that cover the true pose at about 90 percent;
- at least 20 fps on one GPU.
Optional CoRL strengthener: replay tracked object trajectories in a physics simulator with a retargeted dexterous hand, and report replay success against baseline trajectories (sim only, no robot arm).

fit.what_a_small_lab_cannot_do_here:
A small lab cannot:
- build a mocap-grounded fast hand-object dataset at HOT3D or ARCTIC scale, or run a dense high-speed multi-view studio with dozens of synchronised cameras;
- capture many subjects at scale (tens of subjects and hundreds of hours);
- run egocentric extreme-condition capture at EgoXtreme scale (about 1.3M Aria frames across maintenance, sports and smoke-filled rescue), including safe smoke and sports scenarios;
- build stereo or multi-event-camera rigs, or do synthetic event pretraining at Event6D scale (feasible only reduced);
- capture fast athletic full-body or body-object motion with metric ground truth;
- train foundation-scale blur-robust hand or object models;
- validate on a robot without borrowing an arm.
The realistic scope is:
- a 40-80 sequence desk-scale benchmark with pseudo ground truth from 2-3 cheap high-speed global-shutter cameras;
- a model-based or lightly learned method on one GPU;
- careful accounting of ground-truth error and of the interpolation baseline.

A small lab cannot:
- collect glove-tactile data at the scale the field now uses (OpenTouch 5.1 h, H-Tac 160 h, TouchSight 500 h), or build custom high-density tactile gloves (ART-Glove has 2048 taxels);
- train video-diffusion pressure models such as EgoTactile or EgoPressDiff from scratch on one GPU;
- build dense-taxel instrumented objects with full-surface coverage like EgoPHI's, or measure shear across the whole bare hand;
- show real-robot force transfer (ForceBand 87 percent success, Dex-X, CHORD) without a borrowed robot;
- claim in-the-wild or many-object generality from one to three instrumented objects;
- do body-level force work, having no force plates, insoles or EMG;
- compete on the generic RGB-to-pressure-map task, where ETH (Holz), MIT, SNU (Joo), Tsinghua (Ding) and Meta have data and compute advantages.
Even the feasible plan carries a limit: the benchmark will be one rig, one or two objects and about ten subjects, so its claims must stay bounded to rigid objects, desk-scale bimanual handling and the measured force components (normal force, plus shear only if 6-axis sensors are affordable).

Verdict context: the topic is MAYBE because the narrow core is real and open. That core is joint recovery of a body with changed topology plus an attached rigid device, and above all the upper-limb manipulation case, which my arXiv queries found unaddressed. Its feasibility, however, hinges on a clinical partner. A small lab cannot:
(1) recruit a meaningful number of upper-limb prosthesis users without a clinic partnership and ethics approval;
(2) obtain marker-based mocap ground truth on residual limbs and sockets (RSP3D needed a multi-camera mocap lab and athletes);
(3) learn a new topology-variable body model from hundreds of scans;
(4) match large-scale synthetic training such as Anny-One's 780k images, the AJAHR A3D set, or SAM 3D Body-scale training;
(5) cover the combinatorial space of amputation levels, sockets and devices;
(6) compete on the lower-limb or running-prosthesis version, where the UTokyo group already holds the data and has named the next step;
(7) do loose-clothing studio capture, which needs multi-view domes. That sub-problem should be dropped anyway, as both skeptics concluded.
Also drop impaired motion with intact topology: arXiv 2606.17427 reports no significant HaMeR or WiLoR accuracy gap for cervical spinal-cord-injury hands. Without a partner, only RQ2 and a simulator-only RQ1 remain. Each is publishable as a method paper but weaker at a top venue.
Verification note: the one WebSearch call I attempted was refused because the shared budget was exhausted. Paper claims were checked through arXiv abstract pages and the arXiv API only. I did not verify LDPose, InclusiveVidPose, Care-PD, AJAHR's venue, or the release status of any dataset.
